  "worked_solution": "To estimate $\\sqrt{45},$ we first find the two perfect squares closest to $45.$\n\nThe closest perfect squares are $36={6}^{2}$ and $49={7}^{2}.$\n\nSince $36<45<49,$ we know that the square root of $45$ lies between $6$ and $7.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14239128/assets/f9d2536ecb1a4d163997e6415b21ff95094074c7fb521a535fc41d33bb0f0173.png)\n\nSince $45$ lies between $36$ and $49,$ we expect $\\sqrt{45}$ to lie between $6$ and $7.$\n\nLet's test decimals near the middle by squaring them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6.7}^{2} & =44.89 \\\\ {6.8}^{2} & =46.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $45$ lies between $44.89$ and $46.24,$ so $6.7<\\sqrt{45}<6.8.$\n\nNext, we determine which value is closer to $45:$\n\n- The distance from $45$ to ${6.7}^{2}$ is $45-44.89=0.11.$\n\n- The distance from $45$ to ${6.8}^{2}$ is $46.24-45=1.24.$\n\nSince $45$ is much closer to $44.89,$ $\\sqrt{45}$ is closer to $6.7.$\n\nTherefore, rounded to $1$ decimal place, $\\sqrt{45}≈6.7.$",
